{0 Chrestomathy}

{1 Ada}

- specializes for macOS by having the Makefile alter a constant in [build_options.ads] with Perl after checking [uname]
- does a style check with [gnatpp] which... usually just causes a build failure, because [gnatpp] isn't part of the normal GNAT distribution.
- checks [getrusage(2)] constants with a C program that asserts Ada's understanding
- for comfortable editing, needs project spellings/case in [dict]
- nine [with] module imports, and four [use] module inclusions.
- [struct rusage], [getrusage], [posix_spawn], [waitpid], are all tediously (but exactly) handled with C FFI and without writing C.
- unbuffered I/O
- bare "syscall failed" error messages

I don't entirely hate this code.

{1 ATS}

- C wrapping is nicer than every alternative, very precisely describing C resource use with very little code.
- but also inlines a large chunk of C just to have less painful output
- otherwise this code is not showing ATS off at all

{1 C}

C has the easiest time of this task and still mostly shows off C's
disadvantages, with subtle bugs and unchecked errors.

{1 D}

This D isn't taking advantage of ImportC or interpolated strings but is still fairly clean.

{1 Forth}

- requires a [.so] and cheats a bit with FFI
- unbuffered I/O, though easily fixed
- uniquely bad in that units aren't changed

Overall, poor code. There's a serious cognitive cost from Forth and here
there's no benefit to compensate for that.

{1 Mercury}

Overcomplicated (by me) with state variables, and the FFI's somewhat gross, but
there's a lot to like about the code. It's not accidental that this version has
so many features.

Mainly, it looks bad. Pattern-matching, records, the syntax in general compares
very unfavorably to OCaml.

{1 Nim}

- some gratuitously bad style from me: global state, a meaningless
  [isMainModule], weird C [emit] abuse. In these terms even Ada does better.
- nonetheless very clean-looking and maintainable code, having interpolation as
  an advantage over standard OCaml.

{1 OCaml}

- the first version had pretty ugly output with 14 arguments to
  [Printf.printf], including a single giant format string. Breaking that up or
  using {{: https://github.com/EmileTrotignon/ppx_format}ppx_format} helped a lot.
- unique in that it uses a separate C stub that uses the OCaml compiler API.
- unique in that [Stdlib] argument handling could still be used with [getr]'s
  CLI that passes arbitrary flags onto the spawned commands.
- unique (with Forth) in having reasonable interactive development with [dune
  utop].
- opinions vary but I quite like dune, and prefer it to cargo.
- I like odoc, too. Of these languages only Rust and go have reasonable
  competition.

{1 Rust}

{@rust[
fn exe() -> Option<String> {
    Some(std::env::current_exe().ok()?.file_name()?.to_str()?.to_string())
}
]}

I don't entirely hate this either.

