{1 Conclusion}

{{: https://ocaml.org/about}Why OCaml?} provides an argument for OCaml that
isn't very satisfying to the zeitgeist: it doesn't establish a niche or a
unique selling point. It says "sweet spot", "combination ... that is
unmatched", "elegant combination", etc. But this is also my conclusion from
these implementations.

Ada, Mercury, Rust, and Zig (not included due to its instability) all pay a
very large price in clarity and maintainability for both small and dubious
benefits. C, D, Forth, Go, and Nim all pay a price in developer confidence for
small and dubious benefits in developer ease.

Design decisions come with costs and OCaml bears its costs relatively well.
